{-
  Atomic - the substrate a concurrency library is built out of, and not a concurrency model.

  Analysis-Atomics.md §3, and doc/spec/concurrency.md for the memory model the operations here are
  written against. What this module supplies is indivisible access to one location and the ordering
  edges that make one thread's writes visible to another. What it does not supply is threads, a
  scheduler, a mutex or a channel: those belong above it, and every one of them is written in terms
  of what is here.

  § Why a dedicated type

  An atomic is a *location*, not a value. `Atomic(a)` is opaque storage whose only construction is
  `atomic` and whose only access is one of the operations below, which is what makes every atomic
  access visible in the source that performs it. The alternatives - a field marked `atomic`, or a
  set of raw-pointer intrinsics - each hide one half of that: the first lets ordinary field syntax
  conceal an ordering, and the second makes the safe surface unsafe. See §8.

  It is never `Copy` and has no `TrivialCopy`, which is the property the whole module rests on: a
  copied atomic is a second location, and two threads synchronizing through two locations are not
  synchronizing. It *is* `TrivialSink`, so it may be moved while exclusively owned - owning it
  exclusively is a proof that nobody is sharing it.

  § Native only, and why that is the file name rather than an attribute

  The whole module is native, so the *file* says so once. JavaScript has no worker or shared-buffer
  model in this compiler and no operation that hands a value to one, so an implementation over
  ordinary objects would be a single-threaded facsimile that conceals a portability error (§5.4).

  `@platform(native)` on every declaration would say the same thing thirty times, and a reader
  adding the thirty-first would have to notice. The file selector is right here for the reason it is
  wrong in `Native/Intrinsic/X86.yana` - there, some declarations are baseline and some need a
  feature, so a selector on the file would hide the ones that belong everywhere. Here there is
  nothing that belongs anywhere else.

  If workers are designed later, the same declarations may be supplied by an `Atomic.js.yana` over
  `SharedArrayBuffer` for the widths the host guarantees - which is the ordinary target rule, the
  semantics staying the same while only the lowering differs. On a JS build today this file is not
  read at all, so the module is empty and every name in it is unknown.
-}
module Atomic

{-
  The family `Atomic(a)` admits, and the arithmetic subset of it - §3.2.

  Class spelling so that a generic signature can constrain them, and closed compiler
  classifications like `TrivialCopy` rather than open extension points: the list is the integers, any
  payload-free enumeration (`Bool` among them), and a raw pointer, and it is closed because every
  admitted operation is **always lock-free**. That is a stronger contract than C++'s sometimes-lock-free generic, and the only way
  to keep it is to refuse the widths a target would have to synthesize with a library lock.

  Lock-free is not wait-free. A load or a native fetch instruction completes in a bounded number of
  its own steps; an operation synthesized as a compare-exchange loop may lose repeatedly to other
  threads. What is guaranteed is system-wide progress and no hidden lock, allocation or scheduler
  call - not fairness for any one caller.

  There is no `isLockFree` query, because a program cannot usefully branch around the mutex used to
  implement its mutex.
-}
pub class (TrivialCopy(a)) AtomicValue(a)
pub class (AtomicValue(a), Integral(a)) AtomicInteger(a)

{-
  The four ordering types - §3.3.

  The repetition is deliberate and is the whole design. `store(x, LoadAcquire)` does not typecheck,
  and there is no relaxed fence that exists only to do nothing, so a diagnostic can say "store takes
  `StoreOrder`; use `StoreRelaxed`, `StoreRelease` or `StoreSequential`" without teaching a matrix.

  An ordering argument must be a compile-time constant. Passing one through a generic helper stays
  possible where the compiler specializes that helper, but selecting an order from runtime data is
  an error: an order changes the generated instructions and the optimizer's constraints, so it is
  not useful runtime state - and requiring it to be constant is what guarantees good code in an
  unoptimized build.

  There is no default. What each order means, in the words a code review needs:

    relaxed          only this location's own modification order; it publishes no other memory
    release          effects sequenced before this become visible to a matching acquire
    acquire          after observing a matching release, what it published is visible after this
    acquire-release  both halves, for a read-modify-write
    sequential       both halves as applicable, plus one total order over all sequential operations

  "Matching" is not "using the same address at some point": the acquire must observe the release's
  value, or a value in its release sequence. A load is not promised to read the newest wall-clock
  value; that phrase has no portable meaning.
-}
pub data LoadOrder = LoadRelaxed | LoadAcquire | LoadSequential
pub data StoreOrder = StoreRelaxed | StoreRelease | StoreSequential

pub data UpdateOrder =
  UpdateRelaxed | UpdateAcquire | UpdateRelease | UpdateAcquireRelease | UpdateSequential

pub data FenceOrder =
  FenceAcquire | FenceRelease | FenceAcquireRelease | FenceSequential

{-
  What a compare-exchange answers - §3.4.

  `Exchanged` exactly when the old value equalled `expected` and `desired` was stored; otherwise
  `Unchanged` carrying what was actually there. Neither form mutates its expected argument and
  neither loses the observed value on failure, which is what lets a retry loop feed the answer
  straight back in as the next attempt's expectation.
-}
pub data ExchangeResult(a) = Exchanged | Unchanged(a)

{-
  Construction and consumption - §3.1.

  An atomic is not implicitly zeroed. `atomic(0)` is already explicit, works for pointers and flags
  alike, and avoids a second initialization rule for generic storage.

  `intoValue` needs no atomic instruction at all: owning the value proves there is no outstanding
  borrow and therefore no concurrent accessor. It is the clean teardown path for a containing
  structure, and the way a test inspects a private atomic after every worker has joined.
-}
pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) atomic(initial: a) -> Atomic(a)
pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) intoValue(->self: Atomic(a)) -> a

{-
  The basic operations - §3.4.

  `self` is an *immutable borrow*, which is the default convention here and is the one controlled
  form of interior mutation the language has: many threads may hold that borrow at once, and the
  only mutation it enables is one of the operations here. It creates no `&Atomic(a)` and no `&a`.
-}
pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) load(self: Atomic(a), order: LoadOrder) -> a
pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) store(self: Atomic(a), desired: a, order: StoreOrder) -> {}
pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) exchange(self: Atomic(a), desired: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a

{-
  Compare and exchange - §3.4 and §3.5.

  `compareExchange` is **strong**: it answers `Unchanged` only when the value it read differed from
  `expected`. `compareExchangeWeak` may answer `Unchanged(expected)` spuriously, and exists for
  retry loops on load-linked/store-conditional machines where the strong form costs a loop of its
  own; on x86 the two lower identically and the flag only stops a caller from assuming otherwise.

  Neither form solves ABA. A word that changed from A to B and back to A compares equal to A, so the
  algorithm has to make that harmless or carry a generation in the compared state - which is one
  real caller for a future `AtomicPair`, and is also why the language's slot handles carry one.

  The failed comparison's order is derived from the requested one by dropping the release half,
  which has nothing to order on a path that performs no write:

    UpdateRelaxed          relaxed         UpdateAcquireRelease   acquire
    UpdateAcquire          acquire         UpdateSequential       sequential
    UpdateRelease          relaxed

  That covers the pairs an algorithm normally wants - release/relaxed and acquire-release/acquire -
  without asking every caller two questions. An algorithm that specifically needs a weaker failure
  path writes `Advanced.compareExchange` below.
-}
pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) compareExchange(
  self: Atomic(a), expected: a, desired: a, order: UpdateOrder
) -> ExchangeResult(a)

pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) compareExchangeWeak(
  self: Atomic(a), expected: a, desired: a, order: UpdateOrder
) -> ExchangeResult(a)

{-
  The explicit two-order forms - §3.5.

  The compiler rejects a failure order stronger than the success order, a failure path that
  synchronized more than the successful one being something no target implements. The legal pairs:

    relaxed          relaxed
    acquire          relaxed, acquire
    release          relaxed
    acquire-release  relaxed, acquire
    sequential       relaxed, acquire, sequential

  Putting these behind a longer qualified name is not a judgment that they are unsafe. It says only
  that the extra degree of freedom belongs in code whose algorithm and benchmark justify it.
-}
pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) Advanced.compareExchange(
  self: Atomic(a), expected: a, desired: a, success: UpdateOrder, failure: LoadOrder
) -> ExchangeResult(a)

pub fn (AtomicValue(a)) Advanced.compareExchangeWeak(
  self: Atomic(a), expected: a, desired: a, success: UpdateOrder, failure: LoadOrder
) -> ExchangeResult(a)

{-
  The fetch operations - §3.4.

  Every one answers the value from *before* the update. The `fetch` prefix says so, matches what x86
  and LLVM naturally return, and keeps the family consistent. Addition and subtraction wrap at the
  stored width.

  There are deliberately no operator overloads. `counter += 1` looking ordinary while being shared,
  atomic and ordered by an invisible default would violate all three of this surface's goals at once.

  `fetchNand`, floating updates, min/max and the saturating updates wait for a demonstrated caller;
  each can be added without changing the storage or the memory model. What is here is what mutexes,
  counters, flags, reference state and bitsets actually need.
-}
pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchAdd(self: Atomic(a), amount: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a
pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchSub(self: Atomic(a), amount: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a
pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchAnd(self: Atomic(a), mask: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a
pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchOr(self: Atomic(a), mask: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a
pub fn (AtomicInteger(a)) fetchXor(self: Atomic(a), mask: a, order: UpdateOrder) -> a

{-
  Fences and spinning - §3.6.

  `fence` orders accesses but is attached to no location. It does not by itself synchronize two
  threads: the surrounding atomic operations still have to establish the relation, and a fence
  between two plain accesses orders nothing that was racing anyway. Its main caller today is the
  last-release path of a reclamation algorithm.

  `spinHint` says the loop containing it is polling. It emits x86 `pause`, AArch64 `yield`, or
  nothing where a target has no useful hint. It is neither a compiler fence nor a memory fence and
  establishes no happens-before edge: a program whose correctness changes when every `spinHint` is
  deleted was already wrong.

  There is no compiler-only fence. Using one correctly needs a language model for signals or
  interrupt handlers that this language does not have, and adding it now would invite its incorrect
  use for memory-mapped I/O - where volatile device access and architecture-specific barriers are
  the actual requirement.
-}
pub fn fence(order: FenceOrder) -> {}
pub fn spinHint() -> {}
